  "summary": "Number systems used in computing — denary, binary, octal and hexadecimal — place values, converting between them (by place value, repeated division and grouping bits), and why hexadecimal is used in computer systems.",
  "theory": [
    {
      "title": "Number systems",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.1"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "A **number system (base)** is defined by how many different **digits** it uses. The value of each digit depends on its **position** (place value), and each place is worth **base** times the place to its right."
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "The four number systems",
          "columns": [
            "System",
            "Base",
            "Digits",
            "Example",
            "Used for"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "**Denary** (decimal)",
              "10",
              "0–9",
              "183₁₀",
              "Everyday counting"
            ],
            [
              "**Binary**",
              "2",
              "0, 1",
              "10110111₂",
              "How computers **store** all data"
            ],
            [
              "**Octal**",
              "8",
              "0–7",
              "267₈",
              "Older systems, Linux file permissions (`chmod 755`)"
            ],
            [
              "**Hexadecimal**",
              "16",
              "0–9, A–F",
              "B7₁₆",
              "A **short way to write binary** for people"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Place values",
          "columns": [
            "Base",
            "…",
            "4th place",
            "3rd place",
            "2nd place",
            "1st place"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Binary",
              "…",
              "2³ = 8",
              "2² = 4",
              "2¹ = 2",
              "2⁰ = 1"
            ],
            [
              "Octal",
              "…",
              "8³ = 512",
              "8² = 64",
              "8¹ = 8",
              "8⁰ = 1"
            ],
            [
              "Denary",
              "…",
              "10³ = 1000",
              "10² = 100",
              "10¹ = 10",
              "10⁰ = 1"
            ],
            [
              "Hexadecimal",
              "…",
              "16³ = 4096",
              "16² = 256",
              "16¹ = 16",
              "16⁰ = 1"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Hexadecimal digits",
          "columns": [
            "Hex",
            "0–9",
            "A",
            "B",
            "C",
            "D",
            "E",
            "F"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Denary",
              "0–9",
              "10",
              "11",
              "12",
              "13",
              "14",
              "15"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "exam_tip",
          "content": "Write the base as a subscript (B7₁₆, 10110111₂) or with a prefix: **0x**B7 or **#**B7 for hex, **0b**10110111 for binary. In exam answers always show the base if there could be any doubt."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Binary and denary",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.1"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Binary → denary",
          "items": [
            "Write the place values (… 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1) above the bits.",
            "**Add** the place values where the bit is **1**."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "1011011₂ → denary",
          "content": "| 64 | 32 | 16 | 8 | 4 | 2 | 1 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 |\n\n64 + 16 + 8 + 2 + 1 = **91₁₀**"
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Denary → binary (subtraction method)",
          "items": [
            "Find the **largest place value** that fits into the number; write 1 there.",
            "**Subtract** it and repeat with the remainder; write 0 in places that do not fit."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "183₁₀ → binary",
          "content": "183 − 128 = 55 → 55 − 32 = 23 → 23 − 16 = 7 → 7 − 4 = 3 → 3 − 2 = 1 → 1 − 1 = 0\n| 128 | 64 | 32 | 16 | 8 | 4 | 2 | 1 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 1 |\n\n183₁₀ = **10110111₂**"
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Denary → binary (repeated division by 2)",
          "content": "| Divide | Quotient | Remainder |\n|---|---|---|\n| 92 ÷ 2 | 46 | 0 |\n| 46 ÷ 2 | 23 | 0 |\n| 23 ÷ 2 | 11 | 1 |\n| 11 ÷ 2 | 5 | 1 |\n| 5 ÷ 2 | 2 | 1 |\n| 2 ÷ 2 | 1 | 0 |\n| 1 ÷ 2 | 0 | 1 |\n\nRead the remainders from the **bottom up**: 92₁₀ = **1011100₂**."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "info",
          "title": "Bits and range",
          "content": "With **n bits** you can store **2ⁿ** different values, from **0** to **2ⁿ − 1** (unsigned): 8 bits → 0 to 255; 16 bits → 0 to 65 535."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Binary and hexadecimal",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.1"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "One hexadecimal digit represents exactly **4 bits** (a **nibble**), because 16 = 2⁴. So one **byte** (8 bits) is always exactly **two** hex digits."
        },
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Nibble ↔ hex",
          "columns": [
            "Binary",
            "Hex",
            "Binary",
            "Hex"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "0000",
              "0",
              "1000",
              "8"
            ],
            [
              "0001",
              "1",
              "1001",
              "9"
            ],
            [
              "0010",
              "2",
              "1010",
              "A"
            ],
            [
              "0011",
              "3",
              "1011",
              "B"
            ],
            [
              "0100",
              "4",
              "1100",
              "C"
            ],
            [
              "0101",
              "5",
              "1101",
              "D"
            ],
            [
              "0110",
              "6",
              "1110",
              "E"
            ],
            [
              "0111",
              "7",
              "1111",
              "F"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Binary → hexadecimal",
          "items": [
            "Split the binary number into groups of **4 bits from the right**.",
            "**Pad** the leftmost group with leading zeros if needed.",
            "Replace each group with its hex digit."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Hexadecimal → binary",
          "items": [
            "Replace **each** hex digit with its **4-bit** binary group.",
            "Join the groups; leading zeros may be dropped."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Examples",
          "content": "- 1011 0111₂ → **B7₁₆**\n- 101 1011₂ → 0101 1011 → **5B₁₆**\n- 11 1110 1001₂ → 0011 1110 1001 → **3E9₁₆**\n- **2F₁₆** → 0010 1111₂\n- **1A3₁₆** → 0001 1010 0011₂ = 110100011₂"
        },
        {
          "type": "interactive",
          "widget": "base_converter",
          "title": "Convert between bases"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Hexadecimal and denary",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.1"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Hexadecimal → denary",
          "items": [
            "Multiply each digit by its place value (… 256, 16, 1); A = 10 … F = 15.",
            "**Add** the results."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Hex → denary",
          "content": "- B7₁₆ = 11 × 16 + 7 = 176 + 7 = **183₁₀**\n- 1A3₁₆ = 1 × 256 + 10 × 16 + 3 = 256 + 160 + 3 = **419₁₀**\n- 3E9₁₆ = 3 × 256 + 14 × 16 + 9 = 768 + 224 + 9 = **1001₁₀**"
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Denary → hexadecimal",
          "items": [
            "**Method 1 — divide by 16:** the quotient is the first digit and the remainder the second (for numbers up to 255); repeat for larger numbers, reading remainders bottom up.",
            "**Method 2 — via binary:** convert to binary, then group into nibbles."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Denary → hex",
          "content": "- 183 ÷ 16 = 11 remainder 7 → **B7₁₆**\n- 92 ÷ 16 = 5 remainder 12 → **5C₁₆**\n- 300 ÷ 16 = 18 r 12 (C); 18 ÷ 16 = 1 r 2; 1 ÷ 16 = 0 r 1 → read bottom up: **12C₁₆**\n- 250 → 1111 1010₂ → **FA₁₆**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Octal",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.1"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": "One octal digit represents exactly **3 bits**, because 8 = 2³."
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Conversions with octal",
          "items": [
            "**Binary → octal:** split into groups of **3 bits from the right**, replace each group with a digit 0–7.",
            "**Octal → binary:** replace each digit with **3 bits**.",
            "**Denary → octal:** repeated division by **8**, remainders read bottom up.",
            "**Octal → denary:** multiply by place values 64, 8, 1 and add."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Octal examples",
          "content": "- 92₁₀: 92 ÷ 8 = 11 r **4**; 11 ÷ 8 = 1 r **3**; 1 ÷ 8 = 0 r **1** → **134₈**\n- 10110111₂ → 010 110 111 → **267₈**\n- 745₈ = 7 × 64 + 4 × 8 + 5 = 448 + 32 + 5 = **485₁₀** = 111 100 101₂\n- Linux permission **755** = 111 101 101 → owner rwx, group r-x, others r-x"
        },
        {
